  "content": {
    "id": "p31-q25-30-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Solve the following.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p31-q25",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\log_3(x^2)=4$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\pm9$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x^2&=3^4\\\\x^2&=81\\\\x&=\\pm9\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe original domain is $x\\ne0$. Both values give $x^2=81>0$, so both are valid.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q26",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\log_2(x^2-5)=2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\pm3$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x^2-5&=2^2\\\\x^2-5&=4\\\\x^2&=9\\\\x&=\\pm3\\end{align*}$$\n\nBoth values give $x^2-5=4>0$, so both satisfy the original domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q27",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\log_3(x-4)+\\log_3(x+4)=2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p31-q27-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=5",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p31-q27-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Combine the logs; the original domain ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x>4",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " excludes the negative root.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The original arguments require $x-4>0$ and $x+4>0$, so $x>4$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_3\\bigl((x-4)(x+4)\\bigr)&=2\\\\(x-4)(x+4)&=3^2\\\\x^2-16&=9\\\\x^2&=25\\\\x&=\\pm5\\end{align*}$$\n\nOnly $x=5$ satisfies $x>4$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q28",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$\\log_3(x-1)+\\log_3(x+1)=2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p31-q28-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=\\sqrt{10}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p31-q28-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Combine the logs and retain the root satisfying ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x>1",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The original arguments require $x-1>0$ and $x+1>0$, so $x>1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_3\\bigl((x-1)(x+1)\\bigr)&=2\\\\(x-1)(x+1)&=3^2\\\\x^2-1&=9\\\\x^2&=10\\\\x&=\\pm\\sqrt{10}\\end{align*}$$\n\nOnly $x=\\sqrt{10}$ satisfies $x>1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q29",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$\\log_{10}(x^2+1)-2\\log_{10}(x)=1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p31-q29-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=\\frac13",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p31-q29-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use the power and quotient laws; the original domain ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x>0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " excludes the negative root.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Since $x^2+1>0$ for every real $x$ and $\\log_{10}(x)$ requires $x>0$, the original domain is $x>0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_{10}(x^2+1)-\\log_{10}(x^2)&=1\\\\\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{x^2+1}{x^2}\\right)&=1\\\\\\frac{x^2+1}{x^2}&=10\\\\x^2+1&=10x^2\\\\9x^2&=1\\\\x^2&=\\frac19\\\\x&=\\pm\\frac13\\end{align*}$$\n\nOnly $x=\\frac13$ satisfies $x>0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 88
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q30",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$\\log_{10}(x+1)-\\log_{10}(x-8)=1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p31-q30-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=9",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p31-q30-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use the quotient law and check ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x>8",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The original arguments require $x+1>0$ and $x-8>0$, so $x>8$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{x+1}{x-8}\\right)&=1\\\\\\frac{x+1}{x-8}&=10\\\\x+1&=10(x-8)\\\\x+1&=10x-80\\\\9x&=81\\\\x&=9\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $9>8$, $x=9$ is valid.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 70
      }
    ]
